# {{ project_name }}

MMIXAL is the application language. `mmixal` is the authoritative assembler and
`mmix` is the authoritative runtime for behavioral tests. The application runs
in the MMIX simulator; host binaries in build/bin are tooling only.

There is no maintained semantic MMIXAL static analyzer in this template. MMIXAL
provides authoritative assembly validation; repository-local guardrails enforce
project policy; simulator-based tests verify behavior.

- Run `just ci` before completion. Every behavioral change requires tests.
- Never add network-dependent validation tools, package restoration, external rules,
  remote APIs, containers, or runtime downloads.
- Do not modify vendored MMIXware. Preserve notices, version provenance, and hashes.
- Do not suppress guardrails or add skip/ignore directives.
- TRAP operations, special registers, and direct `$255` uses are capability-controlled
  by exact configuration allowlists. Remove stale permissions.
- Pure modules must not perform I/O. Only src/lib/io.mms owns runtime TRAP calls.
- Maintain explicit composition order: entry, parse, format, io. Define Main once.
- Do not enable implicit instruction expansion with `mmixal -x` casually.
- Keep long-lived/interface registers named and scratch use explicit.
- Keep stdout/stderr byte contracts deterministic across macOS, Linux, and Windows.
- Do not add host-specific application implementations.
- Build output is disposable. Never commit generated host binaries, MMO files, or listings.
- Do not weaken coverage thresholds to make tests pass. Add tests instead.
- Use the declarative test format and MMIX unit programs, not host-specific test scripts.
- Keep local C parsers bounded, check overflow and child status, and free resources.
- Mutation testing, comprehensive secret detection, and dependency CVE scanning are not provided.

Read README.md and config/ before changing the toolchain or project conventions.
